# Summary
Introduced: the new authenticated wrapper gives the appearance of an authenticated control-plane Unix stream, but authentication is enforced only for storage-node heartbeat refreshes and not for the rest of the RPC multiplexer.
The commit introduces `handle_control_plane_unix_stream_with_auth` and `respond_control_plane_unix_request_with_auth`, which route requests through `build_control_plane_unix_response_with_auth` with an auth verifier. However, `build_control_plane_unix_response_with_auth` only checks the verifier in the `RefreshNodeHeartbeat` match arm. Other sensitive RPCs, including runtime-map reads, `SetPgActingSet`, metadata-transfer acting-set changes, Raft leadership transfer, snapshot purge, and election trigger, are handled exactly as before with no authentication envelope required. A caller deploying the new `_with_auth` handler as the authenticated storage-node/control-plane endpoint would still allow unauthenticated clients that can reach the Unix socket to perform control-plane mutations or retrieve cluster metadata.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Identify whether the new authenticated Unix handler actually routes all requests through a verifier-bearing path.
- [x] Confirm the verifier is enforced for at least the intended heartbeat RPC, so the auth mechanism itself is active.
- [x] Check whether non-heartbeat sensitive RPC match arms consult `auth_verifier` before reading payloads or mutating state.
- [x] Dynamically exercise one unauthenticated sensitive RPC through `build_control_plane_unix_response_with_auth(..., Some(verifier))` and verify state changes.
- [x] Use a debugger trace to confirm the vulnerable execution path reaches the mutation call with the authenticated dispatcher active.
## Report
Validated the finding. Code review showed that `handle_control_plane_unix_stream_with_auth` passes `Some(auth_verifier)` into `build_control_plane_unix_response_with_auth` at `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:5248-5264`, but the verifier is only consulted in the `RefreshNodeHeartbeat` arm at `:5345-5350`. Sensitive arms such as `SetPgActingSet` call `control_plane.set_pg_acting_set(...)` directly at `:5364-5368`, and Raft/snapshot operations likewise proceed at `:5438-5465` without an auth check. I added a minimal in-tree PoC unit test that constructs a real `ControlPlaneUnixAuthVerifier`, sends a raw unsigned `ControlPlaneRpcKind::SetPgActingSet` request through `build_control_plane_unix_response_with_auth(..., Some(&verifier))`, and asserts that the PG acting set is created and the cluster epoch advances. Command output: `test control_plane::tests::authenticated_control_plane_accepts_unauthenticated_set_pg_acting_set_request ... ok`. As a contrast, the existing heartbeat test `authenticated_control_plane_rejects_missing_storage_node_heartbeat_auth` also passes, confirming unsigned heartbeat is rejected while unsigned non-heartbeat mutation is accepted. I attempted valgrind, but it is unavailable in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). I then used LLDB on the debug test binary: at dispatcher entry LLDB showed `request.kind = SetPgActingSet` and an `auth_verifier` argument, then after continuing it stopped at `control_plane.rs:5368` immediately before `control_plane.set_pg_acting_set(pg_id, acting_set)`, with backtrace from the PoC test. This confirms the authenticated dispatcher executes a control-plane mutation without verifying an auth envelope.

